data.content:
Here's the output — `Name` and `Age` are PascalCase as requested.

**Key point:** You don't need a naming policy at all. C# record positional parameters are compiled into PascalCase properties (`string Name`, `int Age`), and `System.Text.Json` preserves property names as-is by default. Just declare the record with PascalCase parameter names:

```csharp
public record Person(string Name, int Age);
```

The final `Program.cs`:

```csharp
using System.Text.Json;

var person = new Person("Alice", 30);

var json = JsonSerializer.Serialize(person, new JsonSerializerOptions { WriteIndented = true });
Console.WriteLine(json);

public record Person(string Name, int Age);
```

> **Note:** Your original snippet used lowercase `name`/`age` — that would generate lowercase properties and lowercase JSON keys. The fix is simply to capitalize the parameter names in the record declaration, which is the standard C# convention anyway.
